     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order as written. I can't verify the specialist recommendation or the chart note. Also, 5,100 mg/day is about double the maximum labeled dose, and metformin has no added glycemic benefit above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day. The extra dose mainly increases the risk of lactic acidosis. A stated rationale doesn't change that risk, so I'd rather not put this through on my own.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals, or 1,000 mg PO twice daily. I can enter either one now.\n- **Get pharmacy and specialist sign-off first.** If a higher dose is truly intended, the specialist's recommendation could be documented directly in the order. Pharmacy could then review and verify it through your hospital's high-dose or override process.\n- **Look at other options.** If standard dosing failed, adding or switching to another agent is usually more effective and safer than raising metformin past its maximum. That's a decision for the specialist and the primary team.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
